First Author: Hsich
Title: Variables of importance in the Scientific Registry of Transplant Recipients database predictive of heart transplant waitlist mortality
Source number: 19  ￼

Very short summary: This national SRTR study used Random Survival Forests to identify the most important predictors of heart-transplant waitlist mortality. Five key findings were: (1) 33,069 adult active waitlist candidates were analyzed; (2) during a median 4.3-month follow-up, 5,514 candidates died; (3) the strongest predictors included eGFR, serum albumin, ECMO, ventricular assist devices, mechanical ventilation, peak oxygen consumption, hemodynamics, inotrope support, and heart-disease type; (4) eGFR and albumin showed additive risk, with worse survival at lower values; and (5) important sex interactions were identified, including worse survival in women than men at UNOS 1A/1B when eGFR was roughly 40–80 mL/min/1.73 m². Tables 1 and Figures 1–5 were used directly for this extraction.

